.SH NAME
ns_thread \- Operate on threads
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBns_thread create\fR ?\fB-detached\fR? ?\fB-name \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB--\fR? \fIscript\fR
.sp
\fBns_thread handle\fR
.sp
\fBns_thread id\fR
.sp
\fBns_thread name\fR ?\fIname\fR?
.sp
\fBns_thread stackinfo\fR
.sp
\fBns_thread wait\fR \fIthreadid\fR
.sp
\fBns_thread yield\fR
.sp
.BE
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBns_thread\fR command provides mechanisms to create and
manipulate threads, facilitating concurrent programming within
NaviServer\&.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBns_thread create\fR ?\fB-detached\fR? ?\fB-name \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB--\fR? \fIscript\fR
Creates a new thread and executes the provided \fIscript\fR\&. The optional
\fB-detached\fR flag specifies that the thread runs detached, meaning it
cannot be joined or waited for\&. If \fB-name\fR is specified,
the thread is named in the format \fI-tcl-$name:$nr-\fR, where \fI$nr\fR is a
unique counter\&.
.RS
.TP
Joinable threads (default):
Threads created without
\fB-detached\fR are joinable, allowing the main program to wait for their
completion using \fBns_thread wait\fR\&. These threads must be joined to
ensure resources are properly freed\&.
.TP
Detached threads:
Threads created with \fB-detached\fR run
independently and do not need to be joined\&. This command returns an
empty result for detached threads\&.
.RE
.TP
\fBns_thread handle\fR
Returns the handle for the current thread\&.
The handle is used as argument \fIthreadid\fR in \fBns_thread wait\fR\&.
.TP
\fBns_thread id\fR
Returns the current thread's ID as a hex value\&. This ID matches the
third element in the sub-list returned from \fBns_info threads\fR\&.
.TP
\fBns_thread name\fR ?\fIname\fR?
Returns the name of the current thread\&. If the optional \fIname\fR
argument is provided, sets the current thread's name to the specified
value\&.
.TP
\fBns_thread stackinfo\fR
Returns the maximum available size of the C stack and the currently
free size of the C stack for the current thread in form of a Tcl dict\&.
Example result: \fImax 2048000 free 2024408\fR\&.
.TP
\fBns_thread wait\fR \fIthreadid\fR
Waits for the thread identified by \fIthreadid\fR to complete\&. If the
thread is still running, the caller blocks until the thread
finishes\&. The command returns the result of the script executed by
the joined thread\&.
.sp
Note: Waiting on an invalid threadid or a threadid that has
already been joined will cause the server to abort with a fatal
error\&.
.TP
\fBns_thread yield\fR
Causes the current thread to yield its CPU time, allowing other
threads to execute\&.
.PP
.SH EXAMPLES
Example 1: Creating and Waiting for a Thread
.CS


 set threadHandle [ns_thread create {
   ns_log notice "Thread is executing"
   return "Finished processing"
 }]
 set result [ns_thread wait $threadHandle]
 ns_log notice "Main thread received result: $result"

.CE
Example 2: Detached Thread
.CS


 ns_thread create -detached {
   ns_log notice "Detached thread running independently"
 }

.CE
Example 3: Setting and Retrieving Thread Name
.CS


 ns_thread name "myCustomThread"
 ns_log notice "Thread name: [ns_thread name]"

.CE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
ns_cond, ns_critsec, ns_mutex, ns_rwlock, ns_sema, ns_thread
.SH KEYWORDS
concurrency, multithreading, server built-in, synchronization, thread management, threading
